##DESCRIPTION
##  Problem converts between decimal and base-5
##ENDDESCRIPTION

##KEYWORDS('teacher preparation', 'base-5')

## DBsubject('Arithmetic')
## DBchapter('Other Number Systems')
## DBsection('Base 5 Numbers')
## Date('8/30/2020')
## Author('Peter Staab')
## Institution('Fitchburg State University')
# updated 2023.08.20

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'contextBaseN.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

# start using decimal numbers
Context('Numeric');
$a1 = Real(random(1,   24));
$a2 = Real(random(26,  124));
$a3 = Real(random(126, 625));

# Now switch to base-5. The ->value looks up the decimal version of the number.
Context('BaseN')->setBase(5);

$a1_5 = Real($a1->value);
$a2_5 = Real($a2->value);
$a3_5 = Real($a3->value);

BEGIN_PGML

Convert each of the following from base-5 to decimal:

1. [`[$a1_5]_5`]=[__]{$a1}

2. [`[$a2_5]_5`]=[__]{$a2}

3. [`[$a3_5]_5`]=[__]{$a3}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_HINT
Think about using the chip/coin model. The number [`21_5`] can be thought of as 2 nickels
and 1 penny or 11 cents.
END_PGML_HINT

@d1 = split //, ("$a1_5");
@d2 = split //, ("$a2_5");
@d3 = split //, ("$a3_5");

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION

Recall that from right to left, the digits represent ones, fives, 25s and 125s, so using the digits as multiples of these values:

1. [`5 \times [$d1[0]] + [$d1[1]] = [$a1]`]
2. [`25 \times [$d2[0]] + 5 \times [$d2[1]] + [$d2[2]] = [$a2]`]
3. [`125 \times [$d3[0]] + 25 \times[$d3[1]]+ 5 \times [$d3[2]] + [$d3[3]] = [$a3]`]

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
